response:
We can rewrite the expression as \[\frac{1}{n(n+1)} = \frac{1}{n}-\frac{1}{n+1}.\]
Note that the terms in the expression telescope when we expand it:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{1(1+1)} + \frac{1}{2(2+1)} + \frac{1}{3(3+1)} + \dots + \frac{1}{1000(1000+1)} &= \left(\frac{1}{1}-\frac{1}{2}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{3}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{3}-\frac{1}{4}\right) + \dots + \left(\frac{1}{1000}-\frac{1}{1001}\right) \\
&= 1 - \frac{1}{1001} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{1000}{1001}}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: \frac{1000}{1001}

