We can use synthetic division to divide $6x^3 - 15x^2 + 21x - 23$ by $3x - 6$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{2x^2} & +1x & +4 \\
\cline{2-6}
3x - 6 & 6x^3 & -15x^2 & +21x & -23 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-6x^3} & +12x^2 \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & -3x^2 & +21x \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & +3x^2 & -6x \\
\cline{3-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & 15x & -23 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & -15x & +30 \\
\cline{4-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0 & 7 \\
\end{array}
\]
The remainder is $0x^2 + 15x + 7$, so the remainder when $6x^3 - 15x^2 + 21x - 23$ is divided by $3x - 6$ is $\boxed{7}$.
The answer is: 7

